The development of the \citev{trame-radial-menu} broadly followed the stages of
the Agile methodology \cite{agile}, including planning, analysis, design,
implementation, testing, deployment, and iterative refinement.

Three members of the team acted as product owners, one of whom also served as
the code reviewer.

\subsubsection{Analysis}

The initial requirement was to develop a "Krita palette" for trame-slicer.

The Krita palette provides a rich set of features through a radial menu
displayed around the mouse cursor upon activation (\cref{fig:krita-palette}). It
includes a side panel containing brush options and enables users to switch
quickly between drawing tools. A similar interface could significantly benefit
trame-slicer's medical viewer.

\insertfigure {assets/images/krita-palette} {height=0.3\textheight} {Krita
palette \cite{Krita}} {fig:krita-palette}

The medical viewer in trame-slicer already offers a comparable set of tools,
although they are currently located within a side panel, as shown in
\cref{fig:medical-viewer-ui}.

\insertfigure {assets/images/medical-viewer-ui} {height=0.3\textheight} {Medical
Viewer UI} {fig:medical-viewer-ui}

The first step consisted of conducting a state-of-the-art review
(\cref{appendix:sota-pie-menu}). This review aimed to determine whether existing
solutions already addressed the identified needs and, if so, to evaluate their
features, design choices, underlying technologies, and licensing models.

Most of the reviewed solutions suffered from poor maintenance. Several relied on
frameworks that were either excessively large or incompatible with trame-slicer,
and none provided both the Vue.js modularity and the complete feature set
required for the project.

Since no existing solution fully satisfied the requirements, the decision was
made to develop a custom radial menu inspired by the design and technical
approaches observed in the reviewed systems.

\subsubsection{Development}

The next phase focused on implementing the radial menu in Vue.js. An initial
proof of concept (\cref{fig:first-poc-radial-menu}), together with its API
(\cref{lst:first-poc}), was presented to the product owners. Their feedback
helped refine the design, API structure, and feature set.

In this first proof of concept, the API (\cref{lst:first-poc}) supported
multiple wheels, with an outer \texttt{RadWheel} defined as a child component of
an inner \texttt{RadWheel}. However, the available \texttt{RadItem} types were
limited to text labels and checkboxes. The product owners requested the ability
to embed arbitrary HTML content instead.

\insertfigure {assets/images/poc-api-1} {width=\textwidth} {Components of the
first POC's API of the radial menu} {fig:first-poc-classes}

Feedback regarding the visual design also indicated that the double-wheel layout
was not well suited to trame-slicer. Instead, a user experience and interface
closer to the Krita palette, featuring a side panel for tool options, was
preferred. This feedback led to a more refined design.

\insertfigure {assets/images/radial-menu-first-poc} {height=0.2\textheight}
{Radial Menu first proof of concept in VueJS} {fig:first-poc-radial-menu}

\lstinputlisting[language=vue, caption={Radial Menu first proof of concept's
API}, label={lst:first-poc}, float,
basicstyle=\scriptsize]{assets/code/first-poc-radial-menu.vue}

Converting the Vue.js components into a trame widget was relatively
straightforward, as a Cookiecutter \cite{Cookiecutter} template already exists
for trame and includes support for generating widgets from Vue.js components.

This was followed by a code review phase, during which the code reviewer
suggested improvements to coding style, documentation, and implementation
details.

