\subsubsection{Maintenance Process}

Once development was complete, the project was moved to a Kitware-owned
repository and published to PyPI through a GitHub Actions workflow
\cite{github-actions}.

New feature requests are now tracked through issues. Proposed changes are
implemented through pull requests, which are reviewed by the code reviewer
before being merged, as illustrated by \cref{pr:Kitware/trame-radial-menu/1}.

The project was published on PyPI, making it installable via \texttt{pip install
trame-radial-menu}. It was also added to the trame widget catalog and announced
in the trame news through \cref{pr:Kitware/trame/895} and
\cref{pr:Kitware/trame/897}.

\subsubsection{\refrepo{trame-slicer} Medical Viewer Example}

Since one of the primary target applications for the radial menu was software
such as trame-slicer's medical viewer, integration into this application was
considered an important use case.

The Medical Viewer provides an excellent example of a complex application
featuring both sophisticated user interfaces and substantial application logic.
This section describes its architecture and the integration process.

\Cref{fig:medical-viewer-classes} presents a simplified view of the classes
involved. These classes follow the Singleton design pattern (see
\cite{design_patterns}), meaning that only one instance of each class exists. An
arrow between \texttt{SegmentEditorUI} and \texttt{MedicalViewerUI} indicates
that \texttt{MedicalViewerUI} instantiates and maintains a reference to
\texttt{SegmentEditorUI}.

\insertfigure{assets/images/medical-viewer-1}{width=\textwidth}{Simplified
Medical Viewer's class diagram. These classes are singletons and arrows aren't
inheritance but mean "is known by"}{fig:medical-viewer-classes}

The architecture separates user interface responsibilities from application
logic. UI classes manage interactions with the user through trame components and
store interface state using typed states (\cref{sec:typedstates}). Logic classes
respond to UI triggers and handle communication with Slicer.

For example, when a user selects a segmentation tool,
\texttt{SegmentEditorUI.set\_active\_tool("Brush")} is invoked. This call
triggers the corresponding procedure within \texttt{SegmentEditorLogic}, which
instructs the Slicer instance to activate the requested tool.

The UI is therefore organized as a hierarchical tree, similar to the structure
of an HTML document. Each node corresponds to a UI component and maintains its
own typed state, such as the currently selected segmentation tool. The logic
layer mirrors this same structure. Every logic object exposes a \texttt{set\_ui}
property that receives the corresponding UI object. This registration process
connects UI triggers to their associated logic methods using Signals from the
\definerepo{https://github.com/KitwareMedical/py-undo-stack}{py-undo-stack}
repository.

\subsubsection{Radial Menu Integration in the Medical Viewer}

A personal fork of trame-slicer was created to integrate the radial menu into
the Medical Viewer \cref{repo:trame-slicer/radial-menu}.

Integrating the radial menu into the user interface, adding buttons as
\texttt{RadItem}s, and configuring the side panel proved relatively
straightforward. However, the menu initially appeared on every right-click,
which was undesirable because right-click interactions were already assigned to
other operations, such as zooming or terminating markup placement.

Resolving this issue required intercepting right-click events within Slicer
itself rather than at the JavaScript level. This necessitated a deeper
investigation of Slicer's architecture. The relevant event propagation paths are
illustrated in two scenarios: when a user right-clicks on empty space within a
scene (\cref{fig:slicer-architecture-void}) and when a user right-clicks on a
specific node (\cref{fig:slicer-architecture-node}).

\insertfigure{assets/images/slicer-architecture-1}{width=\textwidth}{Propagation
of a right click in the void of a view in 3D Slicer through 3D Slicer's
architecture}{fig:slicer-architecture-void}

In the first scenario, the event must be intercepted at the level of
\texttt{vtkMRMLApplicationLogic}. Prior to reaching this layer, additional
processing may determine whether the interaction is valid. Beyond this point,
event handling transitions into the Qt interaction layer. In the second
scenario, events must instead be intercepted at the \texttt{vtkMRMLDisplayNode}
level.

\insertfigure{assets/images/slicer-architecture-2}{width=\textwidth}{Propagation
of a right click on a node of a view in 3D Slicer through 3D Slicer's
architecture}{fig:slicer-architecture-node}

As a result, right-clicking in empty space now opens the radial tool menu. When
right-clicking on a node, the logic stores the selected Slicer markup node and
opens a context menu implemented as a specialized radial menu containing only a
side panel. The available actions depend on the type of markup node. For
example, fiducial nodes (annotation points within the scene) provide options
such as delete, select, and unselect.

The final result is shown in \cref{fig:medical-viewer-w-radial-menu}, where the
radial menu is fully integrated into trame-slicer's Medical Viewer. The
integration enables efficient selection of markup nodes and segmentation tools,
adjustment of tool parameters, and interaction with scene elements directly from
the viewer.

\insertfigure{assets/images/trame-slicer-w-radial-menu}{height=0.3\textheight}{Radial
Menu integration into trame-slicer's Medical
Viewer}{fig:medical-viewer-w-radial-menu}
